230:## Wave 006a — Canvas/tool command controllers
243:This wave is the immediate replacement for the original Wave 006 implementation slice required by `architecture-audit/cycle-006-ui-command-controllers/scope-check.md`. It is limited to the active canvas/tool surface. `ActionsPanel.vue` and `Toolbar.vue` are explicitly excluded except as named residual command leaks scheduled for Wave 006b. If implementation discovers a necessary change to those panel/toolbar surfaces, stop and run a reviewed plan-edit mini-cycle before broadening this wave.
251:The active canvas/tool path has Vue components and tool interaction classes emit typed user intents, collected arguments, and interaction state while application use-cases/tool controllers own command construction, validation orchestration, command-stack execution, commit/rollback, feasibility checks, undo/redo lifecycle, point movement, and style update for canvas-driven construction. Tool/factory modules touched by that path have a coherent application-owned location and dependency direction. `ActionsPanel.vue` and `Toolbar.vue` remain known, intentional residuals for Wave 006b rather than accidental leftovers.
261:- Do not delete or partially patch `ActionsPanel.vue`/`Toolbar.vue` command leaks in 006a; those are the explicit Wave 006b targets.
270:- `ActionsPanel.vue` and `Toolbar.vue` direct command/operation construction are explicitly cited in the implementation log as Wave 006b residuals if still present.
274:Depends on Waves 001–005 so command, process, persistence, and authoritative state boundaries are stable before moving canvas/tool command construction. Wave 006b depends on completion of this wave.
278:## Wave 006b — Panel/toolbar command-intent cleanup
291:This wave begins only after Wave 006a completes and its implementation log records any remaining panel/toolbar command leaks. It must not reopen the canvas/tool controller extraction except for consuming the stable application command/use-case boundary created in 006a. If the toolbar example construction is judged debug-only, prefer deletion over migrating a dead shortcut.
320:Depends on Wave 006a so panel/toolbar cleanup can consume the stable command-execution/use-case boundary established for canvas/tools. Wave 007 remains after 006a and 006b so rendering selectors are not extracted while unresolved central UI command-controller leaks remain.
324:## Wave 007 — Extract 2D rendering selectors and renderer adapters
395:Depends directly on Waves 003 and 007 so 3D rendering does not depend on deleted store paths and can share selector patterns with 2D rendering; through Wave 007 it also transitively depends on Waves 001–006 and their cleaned domain/use-case boundaries.
789:## Wave 016 — Canvas presentation read ports and overlay interaction boundary
810:This wave replaces the now-invalid old Wave 007/008/009 premises rather than continuing them literally. The old Wave 007 named `PaperService.ts` and `RenderService.ts`, but current scout evidence says those files are absent and the active rendering path is `SVGRenderer.ts` plus `canvasPresentation/*`. The old Wave 008 assumed a large `ThreeView.vue` extraction, but current `ThreeView.vue` is already a small runtime shell. The old Wave 009 app-shell god-component premise is also mostly invalidated by the current `App.vue` runtime/action composition. Therefore this wave is the next central post-Wave-015 source boundary: active canvas presentation still receives full `World`, contains `any` seams, embeds projected-3D-on-2D ownership, and has user-observable overlay pointer interception that validation previously routed around.
